-- What a generic signature's non-class constraints promise, checked where the promise is made
-- rather than where the body happens to use it.
--
-- Both kinds are proved at the instantiation site, alongside the class constraints. A declaration
-- that accepted every argument type and left the rejection to the body would report at the wrong
-- place, would only ever report for the members the body reaches, and would say nothing at all
-- about a body that reaches none of them - which is what every body here is written to show: not
-- one of them touches the thing its signature requires.

data Point {y: Int}
data Named {name: Bool}

-- No field of that name.
fn (a.x: Int) getX(p: a) -> Int = 1

-- The field exists and is the wrong type. A property witness reads and writes the owner's storage,
-- so this is not a conversion the caller could paper over.
fn (a.name: Int) getName(p: a) -> Int = 1

-- A function constraint satisfied by nothing of that name.
fn (missing: (a) -> Int) callMissing(p: a) -> Int = 1

-- And one satisfied by a function of that name with a different signature.
fn narrow(flag: Bool) -> Int = 1
fn (narrow: (a) -> Int) callNarrow(p: a) -> Int = 1

-- One instantiation of each. The results go nowhere: what is being asserted is that the call is
-- rejected, and binding a rejected call's result would add a second diagnostic about the binding.
fn main() -> Int:
    getX(Point {y: 4})
    getName(Named {name: True})
    callMissing(Point {y: 4})
    callNarrow(Point {y: 4})
    return 0
